# Authentication module

Installed by `pfw add auth` or `pfw new -template api -auth`.
The application owns all generated source files and can extend `auth.Principal`,
replace the extractor/authenticator/resolver or choose other credentials.

- `GET /auth/me`: requires a valid session; returns the application principal.
- `GET /auth/status`: accepts anonymous requests; rejects invalid credentials.
- Existing application routes retain their access rules. Protect the routes you
  choose with `auth.Middleware.Required` (stdlib) or
  `pfwecho.RequiredSecurity` (Echo), and enforce resource policies in services.

## Run locally

```sh
go tool pfw generate -env local
go test ./...
go tool pfw run -env local
curl -i http://localhost:8080/auth/me
curl -b 'session=local-demo-session' http://localhost:8080/auth/me
curl http://localhost:8080/auth/status
```

The public `local-demo-session` fixture exists only in the local memory adapter
and expires one hour after initialization. It is never seeded into SQL.
Set `APP_AUTH_COOKIE_NAME` to change the cookie name (default: `session`).

## Staging and production

```sh
go tool pfw migrate up -env staging
go tool pfw generate -env staging
```

The migration creates `pfw_auth_users` and `pfw_auth_sessions`. The SQL adapter
loads the user and roles on each request; session lookup uses the hexadecimal
SHA-256 hash of the cookie value, expiration and revocation. The session issuer
must create unpredictable session values, store only their hashes and issue
appropriately secured cookies. SQL sessions have no defaults or demo users.

Login, passwords and session issuance belong to the application and are not
generated by this recipe. This module supplies the request authentication setup.
When protecting state-changing endpoints with cookies, configure the
application's cross-origin/CSRF protection too.

`internal/bootstrap/auth_module.go` contains the environment-specific repository bindings
and the instantiated generic security bindings. The main bootstrap discovers
annotated constructors. The shared HTTP mapper handles 401/403 without exposing
internal errors. Repeating `pfw add auth` does not overwrite this module.
